# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THIS REPOSITORY IS PUBLIC. A mistake here is not recoverable by deletion —
# what was cloned, cached or indexed stays cloned, cached and indexed.
#
# The content directories are therefore DENY-BY-DEFAULT with named exceptions.
# A denylist is the wrong shape for a public tree: it protects against the leak
# classes you thought of, and a new file is published by `git add -A` unless
# somebody remembered to name it. An allowlist fails closed — a new document is
# invisible to git until it is deliberately added here, which is a decision
# somebody has to make rather than one they have to remember not to forget.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── ROOT: deny by default. Added 2026-08-23 ─────────────────────────────────
# ⛔ THE BLOCK BELOW WAS DENY-BY-DEFAULT ONLY *INSIDE* THE DIRECTORIES IT NAMES.
# A NEW TOP-LEVEL DIRECTORY FELL THROUGH TO THE DENYLIST AND WAS PUBLISHABLE BY
# `git add -A`. Measured 2026-08-23 with `git check-ignore --no-index`:
# `docs/anything.md` and `notes/whatever.md` both came back NOT IGNORED, while
# `method/newfile.md` and `bin/newtool` were correctly ignored. The header above
# claims a new document is invisible until deliberately added; that was true one
# level down and false at the root, which is the level a new category arrives at.
#
# This rule closes it. `/*` excludes every top-level entry; the eight below are
# re-admitted by name, and they are exactly what `git ls-files | cut -d/ -f1`
# yields today. A NEW top-level path is now invisible to git until someone adds
# it here, which is a decision rather than an omission.
#
# ⚠ The directories are re-admitted as `!/dir/` — git cannot re-include anything
# under an excluded DIRECTORY, so without these the per-directory allowlists
# below would be unreachable and the repo would publish nothing.
/*
!/bin/
!/contract/
!/lib/
!/method/
!/scripts/
!/.gitignore
!/LICENSE
!/README.md

# ── Content directories: deny by default, allow by name ─────────────────────
# Note `dir/*` and not `dir/` — git cannot re-include a file whose parent
# directory is excluded, so the exceptions below would never be reachable.
method/*
!method/LYING-PROBES.md
!method/TOOL-TEST-METHOD.md
!method/CODING-RULES.md

contract/*
!contract/RULES.md
!contract/REGRESSION.md

bin/*
!bin/bpffs-mount
!bin/dbus-start
!bin/androute
!bin/android-browser
!bin/catch-boot-warn

lib/*
!lib/common.sh

scripts/*
!scripts/scan-secrets.sh

# ── Never commit anything from an engagement ────────────────────────────────
# Belt and braces: the allowlist above already excludes these, but engagement
# output is the class where a single miss is worst, and it is cheap to say twice.
*.pcap
*.pcapng
*.cap
*.hc22000
*.22000
*.potfile
*.hccapx
SortedEssid*
Captures/
captures/
loot/
results/
handshakes/

# ── Credentials and keys ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
secrets/
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
*.ovpn
wpa_supplicant*.conf
hostapd*.conf
dnsmasq*.conf
.env
*_API.txt
*api_key*
*token*

# ── Runtime and device state — none of this is source ───────────────────────
*.pid
*.rec
*.log
state
state.torn-down
snapshot.json
last-compose.json
run/
var/

# ── Local scratch ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⛔ `*.bak` matches `X.bak` and NOT `X.bak.<date>`, and a DATED suffix is the
# parent project's own backup convention — measured there with `git check-ignore`,
# where `TOOLS.md.bak` was ignored and `TOOLS.md.bak.2026-08-17` was not. The `*`
# suffixes below are load-bearing, not tidiness.
*.orig
*.bak
*.bak.*
*.rej
*.save
*.pre-*-fix-*
*~
*.swp
.DS_Store
__pycache__/

# ── OFFLINE BY OPERATOR DECISION 2026-08-23 — the kernel maps and the ────────
# ── verification log. NOT drift, NOT an oversight. ──────────────────────────
# These three documents stay on the device and are copied to the laptop by hand.
# They are deliberately NOT version-controlled anywhere:
#
#   docs/kernel/KERNEL-CAPABILITY-MAP.md
#   docs/kernel/KERNEL-CODEBASE-MAP.md
#   docs/kernel/KERNEL-VERIFICATION-LOG.md      (no laptop counterpart at all)
#
# Why the rule needs to be NAMED and not left to a generic docs/* deny: the
# decision has a reason a future pass must not have to guess at. They carry
# build-host paths, signing-key paths and the signing certificate's Subject Key
# Identifier; a whole-document scan on 2026-08-23 also found this device's
# carrier-assigned cellular address quoted in the verification log (redacted on
# discovery). scan-secrets.sh never saw any of it, because the gate scans what
# git would publish and git would publish none of these — so "no backup" and
# "never secret-scanned" were one fact, not two.
#
# ⛔ IF YOU ARE ABOUT TO ADD AN EXCEPTION HERE: the ordering is SCAN FIRST, THEN
# ADD. Adding a file is what makes the gate start covering it, so an add-then-
# push sequence stages unscanned content, and nothing enforces that the gate runs
# before a push (TOOL-BACKLOG G-20). Take it to the operator; this was their call.
docs/kernel/
docs/kernel/*
**/KERNEL-CAPABILITY-MAP.md*
**/KERNEL-CODEBASE-MAP.md*
**/KERNEL-VERIFICATION-LOG.md*
